                    iFrameResize({ }, '#exco-iframe-229866');
                }, false); Conway added: “This most often occurs when sudden summer rain follows a long, dry period. “Insurance will typically cover any damage as a result of an ‘act of God’ however, if the tree was on council or private land and was already in a dangerous state, you may be able to recover your excess costs from whoever owns it. “If the tree was clearly deteriorating before it fell, or the owner had been warned and failed to act, they could be considered negligent. “If you think the owner was at fault, photograph the tree's condition, keep any evidence, and raise it with your insurer.” Commenting on parking under trees, Mike Smith from Platinum Motor Group said: “The most obvious risk is storm damage, as even a medium-sized branch can leave dents, cracked glass or damaged paintwork, while a larger falling branch could cause thousands of pounds’ worth of damage depending on where it lands and the type of vehicle involved. “As a guide, UK bonnet repairs can range from around £50 to £150 for minor dents, £300 to £700 for a replacement bonnet and £500 to £1,200+ for major structural damage, while comprehensive car insurance can cover falling trees and branches during storms, depending on the policy.” Smith added: “Drivers should also be careful about parking under trees during windy or wet weather, as weakened branches may not be obvious from ground level. “If a storm is forecast, it is always better to move the car somewhere more open, even if that means walking a little further.” Recommended reading: The expert adds that there are a few more things you should bear in mind when parking under a tree: Smith explains: “Sap and droppings in particular can mark the paintwork, and if they are baked on by the sun, they can become much harder to remove without causing scratches.” He also shared some more spots you should think twice about parking close to: He said you can expect an increased risk of debris, dust or accidental damage or a bigger chance of dents, scratches or broken windows in these areas. Concluding, he advises drivers to be aware of where they are parking: “The safest approach is to look up and around before leaving your car. “If there is anything above, beside or nearby that could fall, blow over, leak, mark the paintwork or limit visibility, it is probably worth finding another space.” Do you have any other parking tips for drivers? Let us know in the comments. Share

